increasing these pollutants in agricultural products. Therefore, consumption of foods
containing heavy metals can lead to health risks for consumers, evidence of which has
been reported in various studies [31]. Even the health risk associated with heavy metals in food can increase the probability of cancer in society [32]. Scientific evidence of
health risks from exposure to heavy metals through various routes, including food and
inhalation, has been reported in recent years, responding to concerns about increasing
concentrations of heavy metals in the environment [33, 34]. Increasing the concentration of heavy metals in agricultural soil will increase the rate of transfer of these pollutants to agricultural products [35]. Also, irrigation with water contaminated with heavy
metals can be effective in the accumulation of these pollutants in agricultural products
[26]. The type of vegetables can also be effective in the absorption of heavy metals from
soil and water. Vegetables are divided into different types based on the type of edible
tissue, including root vegetables, fruit vegetables, tuber vegetables, bulb vegetables, and
leafy vegetables. Cucumbers and carrots are among the most important vegetables in
the diet, which are classified in the fruit vegetables and root vegetables, respectively.
Therefore, the concentration of heavy metals in cucumbers and carrots can differ even in
same production conditions.
Iran is a developing country that has industrialized significantly in recent decades and
has been faced with a multi-fold increase in population. However, agricultural lands
have been developed due to the increasing need for agricultural products in Iran. In this
situation, urbanization and industrial development have led to increased exposure of
agricultural lands to sources of pollution. Therefore, the risk of increasing the concentration of various pollutants, including heavy metals, in agricultural products, especially
vegetables, is a health concern. The aim of this study was to analyze the concentration of
heavy metals in cucumbers and carrots in southern Iran as important vegetables in the
Iranian diet and to assess the health risk of their consumption using Monte Carlo simulation. The results of this study can be used to compare the effect of vegetable types on
heavy metal concentrations and its health consequences.

2 Method
This study was conducted in Fars Province in southern Iran. As shown in Fig. 1, Fars
Province is located at 29.1044° N, 53.0459° E, with a significant portion of it being agricultural land. The industrial development and population growth of Fars Province in

Fig. 1 Location of samples point in studied province

