(2025) 6:966

recent decades have been similar to changes in Iran, with industries and cities expanding
near agricultural land. Vegetables such as carrots and cucumbers consist of a significant
portion of Fars Province’s agriculture.
Seven cities of the studied province were selected for carrot and cucumber sampling.
Carrot and cucumber samples were collected simultaneously from supermarkets at each
sampling point (SP1-SP7). Three separate samples of cucumber and carrot were prepared from each sample point. Two kilograms of carrots and two kilograms of cucumbers were prepared in each sampling. The samples were transported to the laboratory
immediately. The cucumber and carrot samples were washed in the laboratory in a similar manner to the usual method of washing vegetables at home, and unsuitable parts
such as crushed or broken parts were removed [36]. The washed and cleaned samples
were dried at room temperature and 200 g of each sample was weighed and separated
[36]. The weighed samples after grinding and homogenization dried at 105 °C for 24 h
[37]. 500 mg of each sample was separated and digested using 10 ml of HNO3 (CAS
number: 67-56-1) at 80 °C [38]. After cooling, the digested samples were filtered using
a Whatman filter, then the filtered samples were adjusted to a volume of 25 ml using
deionized water and stored for metal concentration analysis [36, 38]. The concentration of studied heavy metals in each sample was detected using ICP. Calibration curves
were generated using standard solutions containing multiple elements. Method accuracy
was assessed using certified reference plant material, with metal recovery rates ranging
between 90% and 105%. To maintain data quality, three standard samples were analyzed
after every ten measurements. Moisture content was determined by weighing samples
before and after oven-drying at 105 °C for 24 h and metal concentrations were initially
calculated on a dry weight basis and later converted to fresh weight using moisture content to each vegetable [36].
The health consequences of exposure to the studied heavy metals in carrots and
cucumbers were evaluated by calculating the Hazard Quotient (HQ) and Hazard Index
(HI) using Eqs. 1–3 [39]. In the first step, Estimated Daily Intake (EDI) was calculated
in mg kg− 1 day− 1. The EDI was calculated using Eq. 1, where C is the concentration of
studied heavy metals in vegetables (mg kg− 1); IR is ingestion rate (kg day− 1) of carrot
and cucumber as 0.0052 and 0.020 kg/person/day, respectively [36], EF is exposure frequency (day year− 1); ED is exposure duration (years); BW is bodyweight (70 ± 15 kg); AT
is (EF × ED) average time exposure.

EDI = (CF × IR × EF × ED) / (BW × AT)(1)
The calculated EDI was used to calculate HQ using Eq. 2 [36], where RfD is Reference
Dose (mg kg− 1 day− 1). RfD Pb, Cd, Cr, and Ni is 0.0035, 0.001, 1.5, and 0.02, respectively
[36]. Finally, the sum of the calculated HQs will form the HI, which if higher than 1 indicates the risk of non-cancerous diseases [39].

HQ = EDI/RfD (2)
