Agricultural communities located near artisanal mining
zones in Niger State—including Shiroro, Rafi, Munya,
and parts of Paikoro—are especially vulnerable due to
exposure through contaminated air, surface water, and
irrigation sources, which subsequently affect adjacent
farmlands (Ibrahim et al., 2021; Usman et al., 2020).
Similarly, in Bida, Mokwa, and Wushishi, declining
soil quality has been attributed to excessive fertilizer
application, irrigation with contaminated water, and
runoff from unmanaged waste dumps (Adewumi et al.,
2022). Increased contamination severity correlates
with elevated risks of reduced crop quality,
phytotoxicity, and long-term health consequences for
local populations.

Beyond food safety, uncontrolled soil contamination
adversely affects soil fertility, crop productivity,
ecosystem
functioning,
and
the
long-term
sustainability of agricultural systems. More critically,
chronic exposure to heavy metals has been linked to
severe health outcomes, including kidney dysfunction,
neurological impairment, developmental disorders in
children, reproductive toxicity, and increased cancer
risk (Tóth et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2021). Addressing
these challenges requires a clear understanding of
contamination pathways, exposure scales, and
differential risks across communities and crop types
knowledge gaps that this research seeks to address.
1.2 Statement of the Problem
Although the Niger State depends on agriculture, there
still are a number of environmental and methodological
gaps that manage to sabotage the initiatives aimed at
guaranteeing soil health and food safety:
i. little detailed information on soil-contamination:
The available literature is more localized and lacks
the ability to give an understanding of the
distribution of contamination throughout the state.
ii. Lack of proper evaluation of food-chain transfer:
Not a lot of research measures the conversion of
soil contaminants to crop accumulation and
dietary exposure of people.
iii. Lack of geochemical-agronomic-health systems:
Combining soil chemistry, plant uptake, spatial
mapping, and risk analysis in a single approach is
not a common practice in most research.
iv. Inadequate
hotspots
of
contamination
identification: High-risk areas in the state of Niger
are poorly geospatially mapped.
v. Community ignorance and lax regulatory control:
The farmers are not well informed on the dangers
of contamination and the policies are not well
implemented.

Despite the growing significance of these
environmental and health challenges, systematic and
comprehensive assessments of soil contamination and
food-chain transfer in Niger State remain limited.
Existing studies are largely localized, fragmented, or
methodologically inconsistent, making it difficult to
establish state-wide contamination patterns or identify
dominant risk factors (Olatunji et al., 2023). Given the
increasing complexity of environmental degradation in
the region, a multidisciplinary approach integrating
soil geochemistry, crop contamination analysis,
transfer-factor assessment, geospatial mapping, and
health-risk modeling is urgently required (Zhang et al.,
2021).
Nigeria possesses one of the largest agricultural land
areas in sub-Saharan Africa, with Niger State serving
as a key food-producing region. However, rising levels
of soil contamination have emerged as a critical
environmental concern in recent years (Adewumi et al.,
2022). Contamination sources include artisanal and
small-scale mining activities notably in Shiroro,
Munya, and Rafi—as well as the intensive use of
inorganic fertilizers and pesticides in agricultural hubs
such as Bida, Lapai, and Mokwa (Ibrahim et al., 2021;
Usman et al., 2020). Rapid industrialization,
ineffective waste management systems, and
agricultural intensification have further contributed to
the accumulation of heavy metals and persistent
organic pollutants in soils.

These loopholes impede successful environmental
management and intervention in a health-related matter
in Niger State.
1.3 Aim and Objectives
Aim
The aim of the study was to estimate the level of
contamination of the soils and to determine the transfer
of contaminants between food chains in the chosen
agricultural communities in the state of Niger.
Objectives
i. The purpose of the research was to find out the
level of heavy metals and other pollutants in
agricultural soils of selected LGAs in Niger State.
ii. To examine the level of contaminant in major food
crops that are grown on these soils.

Heavy metals such as Pb, Cd, and As are among the
most frequently detected contaminants in agricultural
soils across North-Central Nigeria. Their presence is
particularly concerning because they are nonbiodegradable and readily bioaccumulated in crops,
posing serious risks to food safety (Alloway, 2023;
Tóth et al., 2022). Once absorbed by plant roots, these
contaminants are translocated to stems, leaves, fruits,
and tubers, ultimately entering the human food chain.
Studies conducted between 2020 and 2024 across
northern Nigeria have consistently reported elevated
heavy metal concentrations in staple crops such as
