Methods
Drinking well water samples were collected during a national-scale survey in Bangladesh.
The dissolved oxygen concentration, oxidation-reduction potential, specific conductance,
pH, and temperature were measured at sampling with calibrated portable electronic sensors. The arsenic concentration was measured by the silver diethyldithiocarbamate method.

Results
As the concentration of dissolved oxygen decreases, the concentration of arsenic increases
(p-value = 0.0028). Relatedly, as the oxidation-reduction potential decreases, the concentration of arsenic increases (p-value = 1.3×10−5). This suggests that arsenic is released
from sediments into Bangladesh’s drinking well drinking water by reduction. As the specific
conductance increases, the concentration of arsenic increases (p-value = 0.023). This suggests that arsenic is also released from sediments into water by the salt effect.

