2.2.2.Agricultural churns (Fertilizers and Pesticides)
Many inorganic fertilizers, especially those that are
phosphate-based, contain trace levels of Cd and Pb
which build up in the soils throughout the years. The
overuse of NPK fertilizers and pesticides has been
reported in Bida, Lapai, Wushishi and Mokwa. The
residues are left in soils and may find its way into crops
during productive seasons. The degradation products
of pesticides also penetrate soil profiles affecting the
work of the microorganisms and the bioavailability of
contaminants.

2.3.3 Hydrocarbons (PAHs and TPHs)
Hydrocarbon pollution causes the decrease in the
fertility of soil, retards plant growth and combines with
metal ions to change their movement.
2.3.4 Emerging Contaminants
Pharmaceutical residues, microplastics and nanoparticles are also becoming common in agricultural
soils, but in Niger State, only a few data are available.
2.4 Mechanisms Soil to Plant Contaminates
2.4.1 Bioavailability and Mobility.
To be absorbed, the contaminants have to be
bioavailable in the soil solution and then the plants
absorb the contaminants. Bioavailability depends on:
pH
Soil texture
Organic matter
Redox conditions
Cation-exchange capacity
Presence of competing ions

2.2.3 Waste Disposal in the Industry and the
Municipalities.
Disposal of electronic waste, industrial effluents and
solid waste improperly leads to the enrichment of the
peri-urban agricultural soil with heavy-metals
particularly in Suleja. Zn, Cu, Pb, Cr and organic
pollutants leachates enter the farmlands through
surface runoff and seepage.
2.2.4 Hydrocarbon Pollution
Fuel storage tanks, road side oil spills and artisanal
refining processes release polycyclic aromatic
hydrocarbons (PAHs) and other metals into the soils.
Hydrocarbons decrease the aeration of soil and change
the mobility of contaminants.

2.4.2 Root Uptake Mechanisms
i.
The contaminants are accumulated in plants
through:
ii. Passive uptake Movement transpiration stream.
iii. Active uptake: Carrier based uptake across root
membranes.
iv. Apoplastic and symplastic pathways: Direct
movement into root tissues.

The use of contaminated water to irrigate lands is
prohibited.
Irrigation with contaminated surface water brings
about metals and residues of agrochemicals to
farmlands. The communities in the River Kaduna and
River Niger floodplains complain of rising sediment
deposition of heavy metals.

Translocation to Edible Parts No translocation should
occur in the case of the translocation to edible parts.
After penetration into the root cells, the contaminants
are translocated to stems, leaves, fruits, and tubers.
Root crops and leafy vegetables have a higher tendency
of accumulation.

2.3 Types of Soil Contaminants
2.3.1 Heavy Metals
The most commonly reported contaminants are heavy
metals of Pb, Cd, As, Hg, Cr, Zn, Cu, Ni, and Mn. They
are not biodegradable, recalcitrant and toxic at low
levels.

2.4.4 Transfer Factor (TF)
The TF measures the movement of the contaminant in
the soil to the plants. TF > 1 is a high tendency towards
accumulation.
Food crops produced in Niger state tend to have high
values of TFs of Pb, Cd and Zn.

Lead (Pb): It is a highly toxic neurotoxin whose
exposure level is not safe.

2.5
Health
Implications
of
Food-Chain
Contamination.
Translocation of the toxins along the food chain poses
significant threats to the health of the people, primarily
due to the frequent usage of contaminated food
materials in the normal day-to-day diet. The results of
adverse outcomes were recorded in empirical
observations and included:

Cadmium (Cd): Builds up in the kidneys and bones.
Arsenic (As): Cancer causing and associated with
cardiovascular diseases.
Mercury (Hg): destroys the nervous system and
impairs the growth of the fetus.
2.3.2 Agrochemical Residues
Pesticide residues (DDTs, organophosphates,
carbamates) are persistent and thus accumulate in the
upper layers of the soil thereby influencing soil-plant
relationships.

2.5.1 Non-Carcinogenic Effects
Neurological impairment
Renal dysfunction
Reproductive toxicity
