2.5.2 Carcinogenic Effects
Some of the carcinogens are arsenic, cadmium, and a
number of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons. They
have been associated with the following malignancies
being present in consumable crops:
Lung cancer
Skin cancer
Bladder cancer
Hepatic carcinoma

These gaps give validity to the current study and
highlight
its
importance
to
environmental
custodianship and societal wellbeing.

2.5.3 Nutritional Exposure Hazards in Nigeria.
The communities, which rely on the leafy vegetables,
tubers, cereals and legumes grown on polluted soils are
disproportionately affected. The constant intake
usually goes unnoticed due to the infrequent and
therefore intermittent biomonitoring.

3.0 Research Methodology
The research design, location of the study, sampling
methodology, techniques used in the analysis and
statistics used to evaluate the extent of soil
contamination and transfer of food chains in Niger
State has been outlined. The method is followed on the
basis of the accepted environmental-geochemical
directions and the outline of the reference documents.

2.6 Empirical studies of the soil contamination and
uptake by crops.
Recent studies in Nigeria indicate some significant
contamination trends:

3.1 Research Design
A multistage environmental-assessment model
comprising of soil geochemistry, crop analysis,
transfer-factor calculations, geospatial maps, and
human health-risk models was followed. The sampling
plan used was cross-sectional whereby representative
soil and crop samples were collected in the agricultural
and mining-affected areas within the selected LGAs of
the Niger State. Standardized
heavy-metal
quantification techniques of analysis were applied in
laboratory, GIS and multivariate analysis in space and
statistics.

Northern Nigeria (202024): Concentrations of lead,
cadmium and arsenic in the soils near the mining belts
are found to be high with high transfer-factor values of
vegetables.
Niger State: Mining districts have regularly shown a
higher level of metals exceeding that set by the World
Health Organization and Food and Agriculture
Organization; crops like spinach, maize, cassava have
shown a high tendency to bioaccumulate.

3.2 Description of the study area.
The Niger state, which is in the North central
geopolitical region of Nigeria, covers an area of about
76,000 km 2. Latitude 8 o 20 N to 11 o 30 N and
longitude 3 o 30 E to 7 o 20 E. It has a unique wet and
dry season with rainfall averaging 1,200-1,600mm per
annum and the vegetation is mainly Southern Guinea
Savanna. The major economic activity is agriculture
that is facilitated by alluvial and ferruginous tropical
soils.

NorthCentral Region: There is a strong correlation
between untimely use of fertilizers and accumulation
of cadmium in the soil; hence, the most susceptible are
leafy vegetables.
Southwestern Nigeria: Where there is waste deposition
and cultivation are conducted simultaneously, the
concentration of lead and zinc is remarkably high.
However, most of these works are limited in
geographical areas and do not have combined soilcrop-risk modeling.

The major communities to be sampled comprise of
Bida, Mokwa, Lapai, Paikoro, Shiroro, Munya and
Rafi, as these areas were selected due to the intensity
of agriculture, the closeness to the mining activities,
and the known risks of contamination.

2.7 Gaps in the literature.
The literature demonstrates that there are various gaps
that have persisted especially in the state of Niger:

3.3 The population and sampling technique are
3.3.1 Target Population
The population of interest will include agricultural
farmlands, food crops that are usually consumed, and
communities that are likely to be affected by soil
contaminants in Niger State.

i. Lack of comprehensive tests that are able to
bring together soil chemistry, crop testing and
human health-risk analysis.
ii. Lack of geospatial mapping of hot spots
regarding contamination.
