3.3.3 Sample Size
A total of:
- 60 soil samples (topsoil, 0–20 cm)
- 30 samples of crop (leafy vegetables, cereals and
tubers)
were done in the selected LGAs. This is a sufficient
sample size in accordance with environmentalgeochemical guidelines of regional contamination
research.

3.6 Soil Physchemical Analysis
They analyzed the following parameters of soil to
determine their effect on the contaminants mobility:
i.
pH (using a digital pH meter)
ii. Electrical conductivity
iii. Organic matter content
iv. Particle size distribution
v. Cation exchange capacity (CEC)
These properties will give information about
bioavailability of contaminants.
Determination of the transfer factor (TF) is done by the
following equation:
The Movement of the contaminants in soil to crops was
also determined using the Transfer Factor given as
follows:
TF = C plant/ C soil.
where
Croot plant: concentration of metal in plant tissue (mg
kg -1)
Csoil = metal concentration in soil (mg kg -1)
TF >1 means the efficient concentration and possible
health hazard.

3.4 Data Collection Methods
3.4.1 Field Survey
Field surveys were used to locate sampling points, took
note of GPS position, examined the state of farms and
noted probable sources of contamination including
mining pits, waste dumps and irrigation channels.
3.4.2 Soil Sampling Procedure
A stainless-steel soil auger was used to collect soil
samples. Five subsamples were summarized into a
composite sample at every sampling point. To
eliminate contamination, the samples were kept in
labeled polyethylene bags which were then air-dried,
sieved (2mm mesh), and ready to be analyzed in the
laboratory.

3.8 Geospatial Analysis
The GPS position of the sampling points was uploaded
in ArcGIS 10.x. Spatial analyses included:
i.
- Metal concentration interpolation by Inverse
Distance Weighting (IDW).
ii. - Hotspots mapping of contamination.
iii. - Overlay of sources of contamination and
farmlands.
The techniques assist in the visualization of the patterns
of spatial distributions within the study area.

3.4.3 Crop Sampling Procedure
Samples of edible parts of crops frequently eaten by the
people (e.g. maize grains, cassava tubers, spinach
leaves) were gathered at the same locations as soil
samples. The samples were washed with deionized
water, dried in the oven and reduced to fine powder.
Before digestion, homogenised samples were
preserved in the airtight containers.
3.5 Laboratory Analysis
3.5.1 Sample Digestion
Digestion of both soil and crop samples was carried out
in the presence of a combination of nitric acid (HNO
3 ), perchloric acid (HClO 4 ) and hydrochloric acid
(HCl) according to the standard USEPA procedures.

3.9 Human Health-Risk Assessment.
Based on guidelines provided by US EPA and
WHO/FAO, risk assessment was undertaken and
evaluated:
i.
- Daily Intake of Metals (DIM)
ii. - Hazard Quotient (HQ)
iii. - Hazard Index (HI)
iv. - Risk (CR) of Pb, As and Cd carcinogenesis.
The equations involved the concentration of
contaminants, the rate of ingestion, body mass and
duration of exposure.

3.5.2 Metal Quantification
The concentration of heavy-metals (Pb, Cd, As, Hg, Cr,
Zn, Cu, Ni) was measured with the help of:
- Annually, 2.4 million new cases of TB occur
worldwide (CDC, 2017).<|human|>- Annually, the
global burden of TB new cases amounts to 2.4 million
(CDC, 2017).

