4.2 Soil Physicochemical Property
The physicochemical characteristics of the soil texture,
pH, organic matter, exchangeable cation and
bioavailability of the contaminants were greatly
contributed by the soil physicochemical characteristics.
The majority of the soils were slightly acidic to neutral
(pH 5.872), and this range of pH increases the
dissolution of Cd and Pb, thus increasing their uptake
potential by plants.
The amount of organic matter was different in the sites
with the amount being larger in riverine farmlands
(Paikoro and Mokwa) which helped in partial
immobilization of metals like Zn and Cu. Nonetheless,
organic matter was not an effective way to decrease Pb
and Cd mobility in the affected areas of mining, which
is consistent with the recent findings according to
which the efficiency of metal-binding is reduced in
soils that are contaminated by multiple sources
(Danladi et al., 2024).

3.11 Ethical Considerations
Ethical factors involved seeking community approval,
maintaining the confidentiality of the field data and
following
the
environmental-sampling
safety
procedures. The course of this study did not harm any
living organism (humans or animals).
4.0 Results And Discussion
The findings of the soil study and the analysis of crop
contamination, transfer-factor evaluation, pattern of
spatial distribution and health-risk indicators of the
study. The results are compared with the literature
available to give a holistic view on contamination
dynamics and food-chain-transfers in Nigeria, in the
state of Niger.

The textural analysis revealed that the soils are mostly
of sandy-loam and are more vulnerable to
contaminants leaching and quick movement to plantroot zones. Physicochemical properties within the area
of study were therefore usually inclined towards the
availability of contaminants to be taken up by plants.
The analysis of heavy-metal levels in food crops is
important because it focuses on the extent of heavymetal contamination in the food crops that supply
nutritional value to the community.<|human|>The
heavy-metal level in food crops is of great significance
since it looks at the level of heavy-metal contamination
on the food crops, which provide nutritional value to
the society.

4.1 Concentrations of Heavy -Metal in soil samples.
Spatial variability in the concentrations of heavy
metals in 60 soil samples sampled at various locations
across the study area was due to proximity to mining
areas, agricultural activity and anthropogenic
disturbances. The mean concentration of Pb, Cd, As
and Cr was particularly high in Shiroro, Rafi and
Munya LGAs- which are regarded to be the regions of
active artisanal mining. Conversely, there was
moderate contamination of farmlands in Mokwa, Lapai,
and Bida which were mostly related with the use of
fertilizers and agrochemicals.

The 30 sample crops were analyzed, and it was found
that there was high deposition of heavy metals in the
edible tissues. Leafy vegetables (spinach, amaranthus)
were the highest concentrations, and the root crops
(cassava, yam) and cereals (maize, millet) were
subsequent.

At each of the sites, the concentration of lead (Pb) was
often greater than the WHO/FAO acceptable limit of
85 mg kg -1, more so in the mining neighboring
communities. There was also overabundance of
cadmium (Cd) and arsenic (As) in some of the
locations, which confirms that accumulation of
contaminants in the surface soils is persistent. The high
levels of potentially toxic elements (PTEs) are
correlated with the results of other research on the same
topic in North-Central Nigeria (Aminu et al., 2021),
which revealed an enrichment of the metal content in
mining and dump-sites.

The amount of Pb and Cd in leafy vegetables in Shiroro
and Rafi were always beyond the safe level of 0.3
mg/kg and 0.2 mg/kg set by WHO/FAO, respectively.
Root crops showed moderate and alarming levels and
the indication of translocation through contaminated
soils was realized. Samples of maize were relatively
lower but also demonstrated that contamination at
mining-proximate areas were detected.
These results support the previous studies in the north
of Nigeria (Hammed and Usman, 2023) which
identified the significance of leafy vegetables to metal
enrichment because of their large surface area, high
growth rate, and high transpiration.

The spatial heterogeneity also indicates that
contamination is not evenly distributed but it is affected
by local geochemical processes and anthropogenic
pressures and thus requires a focused intervention.
