Comparison with WHO/FAO allowable limits showed
the following trends:

The large TF in the vegetables establishes the fact that
the metals in the polluted soils are highly bioavailable
and that the metals may be ingested directly in the diet.
These findings are consistent with the results of like
research in semi-arid agricultural areas where mining
and agriculture co-exist (Ibrahim et al., 2022).

•Over 60 per cent of soil samples and 50 per cent of
crop samples contained safe amounts of Lead (Pb).
• Cadmium (Cd) was above mining and fertilizer rich
areas.
• The levels of arsenic (As) were high around mines.
Zinc (Zn) was over localized enrichment, although it
was within acceptable ranges.

4.5 Geographical distribution of contaminants.
Geocentric analysis with the help of ArcGIS proved
that geocentric jots of contamination were located
mainly in the following areas:
• Shiroro (mining areas and farms impacted by tailing)
• Rafi (craft mining settlements)
• Munya (ancient mining regions)

Such overages highlight the ecological and health
consequences of the long-term exposure to
contaminants.

The reason was the moderate contamination of zones
in Bida, Lapai, and Wushishi, which was mostly due to
the application of fertilizers and pesticides. The maps
created by the IDW showed a good gradient of
contamination as they receded further away, distant of
the mines. The spatial distribution indicates the pointsource and diffuse processes of contamination in the
environment and emphasizes the need to conduct
environmental monitoring in a coordinated manner.

4.8 Discussion
The results demonstrate a clear trend of environmental
pollution which is mostly caused by artisanal mining,
excessive use of agrochemicals, and poor waste
disposal. Niger State had soil physicochemical
parameters that promoted the mobility and uptake of
heavy-metals leading to quantifiable accumulation in
food crops that are widely consumed. The transfer
factors observed confirm that some crops are excellent
bioaccumulators and early warning of environmental
pollution especially leafy vegetables. The health-risk
profile brings out the consequences of chronic
exposure, as both the non-carcinogenic and
carcinogenic risks are increased among the people who
live in the mining nearby communities.

4.6 Human Health-Risk Assessment.
The health-risk assessment showed that the residents
have different degrees of possible exposure:
4.6.1 Non-Carcinogenic Risk (Hazard Quotient HQ)
Children always had higher values of hazard quotient
compared to adults which were attributed to lower
body weight and increased ingestion. In miningaffected communities, the HQ values of Pb, Cd and As
were above 1 that suggested considerable noncarcinogenic health hazards.

All of these findings show that there is a pressing need
towards:
• Stricter environmental control.
•Remediation and rehabilitation of soil.
• Protective management of agricultural inputs.
• Sensitization at the community level.
• Frequent observation of the quality of soil and crop.

4.6.2 Carcinogenic Risk (CR)
The estimate of the carcinogenic risk of As and Cd in
various sites was within the acceptable range (1x10-6 1x10-4) or marginally above. This trend indicates the

The results are rather consistent with the international
literature and support the importance of the balanced
